DECnet-Term for the Amiga
=========================

dnterm.device lets an Amiga terminal program log in to VAX/VMS and
other DECnet hosts over the network, with CTERM (the protocol behind
VMS "SET HOST") or LAT (the protocol of DEC terminal servers). The
terminal program sees a modem on a serial port: it dials a node name
or a LAT service instead of a phone number.

The other way round, the optional maintenance login lets the DECnet
nodes you name log in to an Amiga Shell on this Amiga (see "Maintenance
login" below; it is off unless you enable it).


Requirements
------------
- DECnet for the Amiga, installed and running (LIBS:decnet.library,
  dnetd)
- a terminal program that can use another device than serial.device,
  e.g. Term or NComm
- on the host: a CTERM server (VMS has one built in), or for LAT a LAT
  service (VMS with LAT started, terminal servers)
- for LAT: DECnet with dnetd 0.7 or newer


Installation
------------
Double-click "Install-DECnet-Term". It copies dnterm.device to DEVS:,
the commands dntermpatch and latd to DECnet:C and the scripts
DECnet-Start-Term and DECnet-Stop-Term to DECnet:S, and asks whether to
enable the maintenance login. LAT needs DECnet with dnetd 0.7 or newer:
update DECnet first if yours is older.
From the Shell: cd to this drawer, then

  Installer Install-DECnet-Term

On a German AmigaOS 3.x the installer speaks German, else English.
LiesMich is this text in German.

To remove it, start Install-DECnet-Term again and choose "Remove";
that also switches the maintenance login off.


Using it
--------
In the terminal program's serial settings, choose the device
dnterm.device and unit 0 instead of serial.device. Baud rate, parity
and handshake do not matter. Then type in the terminal window:

  ATD VAX1        log in to the node VAX1 (a name from your node
                  list ENV:DECnet/nodes)
  ATD 1.10        the same with the node's address

"CONNECT" means the link is up, and the host asks for user name and
password. When you log out, the device answers "NO CARRIER". A phone
book entry works too: give the node name as the phone number; ATDT and
ATDP in front of it are fine.

Units 0 to 3 are separate lines: to keep two sessions open at the same
time, start a second terminal program on unit 1.


LAT
---
Units 4 to 7 of dnterm.device reach LAT services instead of DECnet
nodes. Choose unit 4 in the terminal program, then dial the service:

  ATD VAX1        log in to the LAT service VAX1

On any unit the protocol can also be chosen when dialling: LAT or
CTERM in front of the name holds for that one call; afterwards the
unit dials as usual again.

  ATD LAT VAX1    LAT service VAX1, also on units 0 to 3
  ATD CTERM VAX1  node VAX1 over CTERM, also on units 4 to 7

At least one blank goes between the word and the name; case does not
matter. In a phone book, give e.g. "LAT VAX1" as the number. This
helps most with programs that have a fixed unit, such as VAXterm (see
below).

VMS systems with LAT offer a service named after the node; terminal
servers and other systems offer theirs under other names. To see the
services on your network, type in a Shell:

  latd LIST

latd runs from the start of DECnet on (DECnet:S/DECnet-Start-Term) and
listens to the service announcements. Right after the start of DECnet
some services are not known yet: nodes announce them only every 30
to 60 seconds. A dial waits up to 30 seconds for the service.

"Unknown LAT service" means that no node offers that name. Units 4 to
7 are separate lines like units 0 to 3; sessions to the same node share
one LAT circuit. VMS shows the line as "LAT Server/Port: MYAMI/LAT4"
(your node name from ENV:DECnet/Name, here MYAMI, and the unit, also
with ATD LAT on units 0 to 3). A BREAK of the terminal program is sent to the host.

LAT, like CTERM, does not encrypt: everything you type, passwords
included, can be read by anyone who watches the network.

latd writes to T:latd.log. "latd STOP" ends it until DECnet starts
again; DECnet-Stop stops it first, so that open sessions end properly.


Term
----
Term 4.8 by Olaf Barthel is on Aminet as comm/term/Term.lha. Unpack the
whole archive into one drawer and start term from there: it adds the
drawers Fonts and Libs next to it to FONTS: and LIBS: by itself.

Term needs its font GFX.font (in Fonts) for the VT100 line graphics,
e.g. the frames of VMS MONITOR. It loads the font only when it starts;
without it the frames show as letters (q, x, l, ...).

Choose dnterm.device and unit 0 in Settings > Serial, then save the
settings. The default emulation (ANSI/VT220) answers the VMS terminal
inquiry correctly.


VAXterm
-------
VAXterm 2.4 by Tuomo Mickelsson (Aminet comm/term/VAXterm.lha, from
the Fish disks) is a VT220 emulator made for VMS. It always opens
serial.device unit 1; it has no setting for the device. dntermpatch
makes a copy of it that opens dnterm.device instead:

  dntermpatch VAXterm VAXterm.dnterm

Keep the copy in the VAXterm drawer (it needs its fonts and setup
files there) and start VAXterm.dnterm. It uses unit 1 of
dnterm.device, so a session in Term on unit 0 can stay open. VAXterm
has a keyboard layout of its own (US by default); F3 opens its set-up
page, where the German layout can be chosen. VAXterm.dnterm reaches a
LAT service with ATD LAT and the name of the service.

dntermpatch works on any program that has "serial.device" built in:
it writes a copy and leaves the original alone.


Modem commands
--------------
  ATD node   connect (any key while it dials stops it)
  ATD LAT service, ATD CTERM node
             this one call over LAT or CTERM, on any unit
  +++        back to command mode, the link stays up: one second
             without typing before and after it
  ATO        back online after +++
  ATH        hang up (also: closing the device)
  ATZ        hang up, settings back to E1 Q0 V1
  ATE0/ATE1  command echo off/on
  ATV0/ATV1  results as numbers/words
  ATQ0/ATQ1  results on/off
  ATI        version

Other AT commands are accepted and ignored, so modem init strings do
no harm. Results: OK (0), CONNECT (1), NO CARRIER (3), ERROR (4),
NO DIALTONE (6, DECnet is not running), BUSY (7), NO ANSWER (8). A
short reason comes before ERROR or NO CARRIER, e.g. "Unknown node" or
"Node has no CTERM server".

The carrier signal follows the link, so terminal programs show the
online time and notice when the host logs you out.


On VMS
------
CTERM tells VMS the terminal type VT200, but VMS does not treat the
line as a screen terminal until the terminal itself has answered.
Full-screen programs (EVE, MONITOR, ...) need this command after the
login first:

  $ SET TERMINAL/INQUIRE

Without it, EVE refuses to start ("SYS$INPUT must be supported CRT"),
and MONITOR draws its frames from + - | and prints every update below
the last one, so the screen keeps scrolling. SHOW TERMINAL then lists
"No ANSI_CRT"; after the inquiry it says "ANSI_CRT".

It is best placed in your LOGIN.COM. The terminal program answers the
inquiry, so its emulation should be set to VT100 or VT220/ANSI.

The line editing keys of the VMS command line (cursor keys, Delete,
Ctrl-U, Ctrl-H, Ctrl-E) and Ctrl-Y work as on a VT terminal.


Maintenance login
-----------------
WARNING: the maintenance login gives a full Amiga Shell to whoever
uses an allowed node and knows the password. From there everything
can be done that you can do at this Amiga's keyboard: read, change and
delete every file, start any program. Enable it only if you need it,
and only for nodes you trust.

- Only the machines you name may connect. The installation asks for
  them in two separate steps:
    1. DECnet nodes, for CTERM (VMS: SET HOST): addresses or names
       from your node list, e.g. 1.10 VAX1
    2. then LAT terminal servers, for LAT (VMS: SET HOST/LAT): LAT
       node names, also NAME/PORT, e.g. VAX1 WEBTERM/web.anna (see
       below)
  Separate the entries of a list by blanks or commas. Either list may
  stay empty: that way in is then closed, and only the other one is
  open. With both empty nobody gets in, so the installation offers to
  switch the login off instead. A connect from any other machine is
  refused before anything is asked.
- After the connect comes "Password:" (no echo). Three wrong answers
  end the connection, each after a pause.
- The password is stored as plain text in ENVARC:DECnet/maint-password
  (and ENV:DECnet/maint-password while the Amiga runs). Every program
  on this Amiga can read it.
- DECnet and LAT do not encrypt: the password and the whole session
  cross the network in clear text, readable by anyone who can watch it.
- Never use a password you use anywhere else.

On an allowed node, log in like to any CTERM host:

  $ SET HOST 1.10              (VMS, 1.10 being this Amiga)
  dntool login 1.10            (Linux, from this project)

and type the password. "endcli" ends the Shell and the connection.
Ctrl-C and Ctrl-Y stop the running command. The Shell starts in SYS:
with the command path of the boot scripts and runs S:Shell-Startup
first.

The Amiga edits the line itself, as in a Shell window: cursor
left/right, Backspace, Delete, Home/End (also Ctrl-A/Ctrl-E), Ctrl-X
erases the line, Ctrl-K the rest of it. Cursor up/down go through the
last 20 lines typed in this session. The terminal should be a VT100 or
compatible.

Over LAT: in the second step the installation asks for LAT terminal
servers (by their LAT node names, separated by blanks or commas, e.g. VAX1 TSERV2).
If you name any, the login is offered as a LAT service named like this
Amiga (ENV:DECnet/Name), as long as latd runs. On a VMS node in the
list:

  $ SET HOST/LAT MYAMI         (MYAMI being this Amiga's name)

and on a terminal server its connect command for that service. Terminal
servers not in the list are refused before anything is asked. The
same password is asked, and the session works as over CTERM.

Some terminal servers serve many users, such as a web terminal that
opens a LAT session in the browser. Such servers often give every
logged-in user a port of their own, with the user name in the port's
name. NAME/PORT lets only that one port in: only you, not every other
user of the same server. An example: a web terminal shows up on the
LAN as LAT node WEBTERM and names the port of its user anna
"web.anna". Then

  WEBTERM/web.anna

lets in only the sessions anna opens there. Case does not matter. You
need not guess the exact names: connect once. The connect is refused,
and T:dnmaint.log shows node and port. Enter those (run
Install-DECnet-Term again).

LAT has no addresses: anyone on your LAN can give a terminal server
any name, so the list keeps chance visitors out, not someone who sets
out to get in; the password does that. All terminal servers that see
the LAN see the service. Leave the list empty if you do not need LAT.

dnmaint starts with DECnet (DECnet-Start runs
DECnet:S/DECnet-Start-Term) and writes what happens to T:dnmaint.log:
every connect with the node address (over LAT: node and port name),
and whether it was accepted, and at the start an entry in the DECnet
node list that is neither an address nor a known node name.
"dnmaint STOP" ends it until the next boot. To change the nodes or
the password, or to switch the login off for good, run
Install-DECnet-Term again.

Programs that draw on the screen through the console (such as More)
work; programs that open windows of their own show them on the
Amiga's screen, not on the remote terminal.


Tested
------
Term 4.8 and VAXterm 2.4 on AmigaOS 3.1 (Amiberry), with OpenVMS 7.2
hosts: login, DCL, EVE and MONITOR. dnterm.device also on AmigaOS 2.04
with a 68000. LAT on AmigaOS 3.5 (Amiberry and an A3000) with OpenVMS
7.2. The maintenance login on AmigaOS 2.04 and 3.5 (Amiberry) and on an
A3000 with AmigaOS 3.5, from OpenVMS 7.2 (SET HOST) and from Linux
(dntool login); over LAT on AmigaOS 3.5 (Amiberry) from OpenVMS 7.2
(SET HOST/LAT). Reports with other terminal programs and hosts are
welcome.


Version history
---------------
0.8 (17.11.2026)
  - dnterm.device 0.4: ATD LAT and ATD CTERM choose the protocol for
    one call on any unit, e.g. LAT with VAXterm (fixed unit 1)
  - maintenance login: NAME/PORT for web terminals explained with an
    example; the installer says at the DECnet nodes that the LAT
    terminal servers come in the next step
  - DECnet-Start-Term and DECnet-Stop-Term get the script bit, like
    DECnet-Start and DECnet-Stop

0.7 (16.11.2026)
  - Installer and texts in German on a German AmigaOS 3.x (else
    English); LiesMich is this text in German

0.6 (26.10.2026)
  - first release for testers: dnterm.device 0.3, dntermpatch 0.1,
    latd 0.2, dnmaint 0.5; needs DECnet 0.1 or newer
  - the maintenance login takes a list of DECnet nodes; leave it
    empty for a LAT-only login

Development versions, not released:
0.5 (25.10.2026)  maintenance login also over LAT
0.4 (25.10.2026)  LAT: units 4 to 7 of dnterm.device dial LAT
                  services through latd
0.3 (24.10.2026)  optional maintenance login (dnmaint): DECnet nodes
                  log in to an Amiga Shell
0.2 (24.10.2026)  dntermpatch for programs with serial.device built
                  in (VAXterm); dnterm.device takes short open requests
0.1 (24.10.2026)  dnterm.device: CTERM sessions for Amiga terminal
                  programs


